WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.610
Have you ever
heard the phrases,

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.200
"The results were
significant early.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.400
I guess I can call it now,"
or "My boss is getting really

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.030
antsy.

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:19.400
I think she wants the
test results soon.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.890
They seem directionally
positive,"

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:26.090
or "We really need to
balance statistical rigor

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:29.770
with the needs of the business."

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:33.760
The same flurry of phrases
would always overwhelm the data

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:36.150
scientists in my meetings.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.050
Bad fluorescent
overhead lighting,

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.320
illuminating the battleground
between statistical rigor

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.280
and the omnipresent
needs of the business.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.140
As a former data
scientist turned

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.840
product manager and
self-declared data nerd,

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.690
I see both sides of the story.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.810
I wanted to help bridge the gap
between my business and data

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:59.170
peers by understanding
experimental methods that

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:02.770
allow organization to
run tests more quickly

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:05.260
and maintain statistical rigor.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.260
Today, I'll walk you through
three easy-to-follow steps,

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.480
motivated by my own
experiences at Intuit.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.140
So that you can apply
it to your own use cases

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.750
for running experiments
more quickly

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:19.780
and statistically rigorously.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:23.020
First, we'll talk about how
to take big swings so you

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:27.130
can learn and fail quickly.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:30.280
Two, we'll talk about
what sequential testing is

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:35.410
and how to use it, so that you
can end test early confidently.

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:41.290
And three, we'll talk about how
to use R so that I and you can

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:44.700
share your learnings
with your organization

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.680
even non-data scientists.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.650
In reality, businesses
do need to move quickly

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:50.790
to stay innovative.

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.190
However, the results
of reading a test early

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:56.700
could be catastrophic, leading
to your business decision

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.160
being no better
than a coin flip.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.630
Here is data for
simulated A/A test, a test

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.850
where we have two
cohorts but they have

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:08.280
exactly the same experience.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.460
We know that since these
two experiences are exactly

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.730
the same, there should be
no statistically significant

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:17.630
difference between the two.

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:21.530
However, as you can see,
over a 30-day window,

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:24.400
the P value actually crosses
the significance threshold

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:28.150
a number of times all
due to random chance.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.470
If you were to call
your test early,

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:36.800
you could be reading
the same noise.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.800
And instead of making an
informed data-backed business

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.160
decision, you could simply
be rendering your business

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.610
decision no better than
if you had flipped a coin.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.470
The first big
experiment I ever ran,

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:51.350
I fell prey to
this exact fallacy.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:54.710
We were testing a new version
of a recommendation model.

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.260
And we wanted to test our
recommendation model out

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:57.920
in the wild.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.080
It's an older iteration.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:04.350
This was the perfect
candidate for an A/B test.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:08.510
However, to move our
baseline metric of 3%,

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:11.270
we needed almost
a million samples.

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:14.460
And it would take us
three months to run.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.320
Guess what?

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.200
No surprise, we cheated.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.390
For the recommendation
experiment,

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:24.140
we used three-day connection
rate as a baseline metric.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:26.960
This suffered from being a
bottom of the funnel metric

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:29.600
because not only did the users
have to see the recommendation

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.180
and click on it, then
they had to connect

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:34.040
and also hold on
to that connection

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:38.480
for at least three days for
us to register that as a win.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.710
That led to our metric
being only 2.88%,

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:44.180
which is fairly low.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.750
To make matters worse we were
taking only a tiny swing,

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:51.950
only aiming to move our
baseline metric by 3%.

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:55.400
With such a low minimum
detectable effect 3%,

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:58.130
we needed 12 weeks
or three months

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:01.310
to be able to fully
bake our test.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:02.960
If you think about
it, we were looking

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.708
to move a baseline metric
of 2.88% by only 3%,

00:04:08.708 --> 00:04:10.250
which led to an
absolute-- would have

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:13.940
led to an absolute lift of less
than a tenth of a percent.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:19.279
If we had taken a bigger
swing of 10%, 7.5%,

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:23.000
or even just 5% as our
minimum detectable effect,

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.740
we could have
reduced our test time

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:29.000
from 12 weeks we needed
to just two weeks.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.310
A year later, we ran
a second experiment,

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:33.650
where we wanted to
learn from our missteps

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:35.600
from our first experiment.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.640
In the second
experiment, we wanted

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:39.710
to test automating sales tax.

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:43.610
We wanted to show users
country-specific sales tax

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:45.680
rate for their
region and their

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.690
first time use experience.

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:51.020
And this time, we wanted to
apply the principle of taking

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.190
big swings to our test.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:56.120
This time, we used a metric
much closer to the user

00:04:56.120 --> 00:05:00.410
action, engagement clicks,
with a relatively high baseline

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:02.570
of about 7%.

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:05.120
More importantly,
we declared that we

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.820
were going to take
a big swing and set

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:10.580
our minimum detectable
effect had to be 100%.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:13.940
Meaning, that our baseline
conversion rate, 7%,

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:17.630
had to double to 14%.

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:20.420
This time, we were able
to take our projected

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:23.510
timeline to be only two weeks.

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:27.860
The key idea, taking big swings
to learn and fail quickly

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:30.380
is one of the biggest
levers you can push

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:32.690
to speed up your experiments.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:34.820
But we wanted to do even better.

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:38.570
By taking our experimental
philosophy of taking big swings,

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:42.380
we were able to layer on an A/B
testing enhancement, sequential

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:45.410
testing, which allowed us to
take our two-week test time

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:48.770
and actually cut it in
half to just one week.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:50.780
But what is sequential testing?

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:53.300
Sequential testing is kind
of like doing your taxes.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:58.070
It allows you to not
overspend and waste time

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:00.630
waiting for results when you
could have already called it.

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:02.570
But it also ensures
that you don't

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:05.030
underspend time and
actually call a test that's

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:07.490
not statistically rigorous.

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:09.110
Sequential testing
allows you to take

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:11.670
that-- spend that exact
right amount of time,

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:13.130
such that when
you call the test,

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:15.680
you didn't overspend
or underspend time

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:18.200
to be able to call a winner.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.630
The way that it does
that is by building

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:24.290
in a checkpoint or
multiple checkpoints,

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:27.230
where you can check the
results of your test and peek

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:30.920
earlier than a standard
fixed horizon test.

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:34.850
Sequential testing is also
ideal for software situations

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:37.070
because software gives
us the unique ability

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:39.020
to monitor results
in real time, which

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:41.030
means that any
moment of a peek, we

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:45.720
can go ahead and analyze
the results in that moment.

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:48.440
Additionally, since we have
their experimental philosophy

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:50.540
of taking big swings,
it really lends

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:54.060
itself to sequential testing
because sequential testing has

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:57.140
unique ability to
call winners early.

00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:00.740
For a sales tax test, we
applied to checkpoints-- one

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:05.690
at the 50% mark or half of the total
samples had come in and one

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:09.470
at the 100% mark where our
full sample set to come

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:11.600
in at approximately two weeks.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:13.520
At each checkpoint,
you can see that we

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:15.770
have a z-score
acceptance criteria

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:17.840
necessary to be able to
successfully determine

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:18.950
a winner.

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:21.980
The acceptance criteria are much
more stringent in the earlier

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:24.950
checkpoints to account for
the smaller sample size

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:28.310
that you're using to determine
whether there's a winner.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:30.410
Note that the z-score
acceptance criteria

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:33.530
is greater than the 1.96
critical value, which

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:36.380
we normally see during
fixed horizon testing.

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:38.780
It's there because we're
paying a small price

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:41.070
to be able to peek earlier.

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:42.930
Namely, the price is
that our experiment

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:45.920
is slightly less powerful
in a statistical sense,

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:48.230
so that we may miss the
detection of a true effect

00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:50.040
or win.

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:51.870
In this case, we're
using something called

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:55.800
the O'Brien-Fleming boundary,
which we've chosen because it

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:00.090
tries to minimize that
payment at the end of the test

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:02.580
because we're trying to get
as close to the 1.96 critical

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:06.010
value as we can
with that last peek.

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:08.050
As an aside, if you
want to check out more,

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:09.890
there's many more
boundaries you can use,

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:11.830
which may suit your needs more.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:14.230
I wanted to make building
a sequential test

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:17.500
plan as simple as
possible to abstract

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:19.570
away the statistical
complexities

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:23.940
so that anyone at my
company could use it.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:26.190
To do that, I designed
and built an R

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:30.660
flexdashboard to make creating
a test plan as simple as filling

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:33.640
out just a few data points.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:35.650
Here, you can see
my flexdashboard,

00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:38.669
my sales tax test prefilled.

00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:41.490
We included the
baseline conversion rate

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:47.130
of our primary metric at
7.03, the power we wanted

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:49.230
or 80% here, which
is an industry

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:54.040
standard, statistical
significance level of 5%,

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:57.690
which is also an
industry standard.

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:01.364
And here, we included our big
swing recruitment of 100%.

00:09:04.030 --> 00:09:06.280
And finally, we included
the optional value

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:08.740
of a weekly traffic to help
translate our sample size

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:13.250
requirement to the amount
of time we would need.

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:15.740
Until this point,
these are all values

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:19.520
you would include in a normal
A/B test plan sample size

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:21.280
calculation.

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:23.360
The only additional value
we needed to include

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:27.320
was the number of peeks we
wanted to take during our test.

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:29.120
Since our normal
A/B test already

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:31.710
had an estimated
time of two weeks,

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:33.740
we only wanted to
add one checkpoint

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:36.380
to be able to account for
normal weekly behavioral

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:37.670
fluctuations.

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:41.180
Therefore, here, we include the
number of peeks to be just 2.

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:50.080
At the top, we have three
key numbers available--

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:55.090
the number of peeks or 2 in
this case that we've set,

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:59.840
the total number of
samples that we would need,

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:02.140
and finally the total
amount of time it would take

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:05.350
given the number of samples
and our average weekly traffic.

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:09.460
Both of these numbers are based
on a normal A/B testing sample

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:13.600
calculation-- sample
size calculation.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:17.640
Here, our table shows us the
breakdown of samples needed--

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:21.360
number of days and then
the lower and upper z-score

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:23.610
thresholds for each
checkpoint given

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:27.040
all of our inputted values.

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:30.090
Finally, our threshold
graph also [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:34.090
z-score threshold changes
with each checkpoint.

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:37.210
Ultimately, being able
to use this dashboard

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:39.520
and share out my
test plan, I was

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:43.990
able to go from an earlier
test with one million samples

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:47.140
to only needing 294
samples and taking

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:50.440
half the time of a normal
fixed tries in A/B test.

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:52.360
And instead of taking
three months to bake,

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:55.520
we only need to wait one week.

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:58.090
And instead of needing
the full bake time,

00:10:58.090 --> 00:11:01.780
we were able to have multiple
checkpoints to really get

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:05.180
the right amount of time
to be able to guarantee

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:08.960
statistical rigor
without wasting any time.

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:11.560
And finally, the most
important of all,

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:14.230
remove the temptation
to cut corners.

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:16.060
And instead, we're
able to guarantee

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:20.170
statistically rigorous results
at shorter time points.

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:21.760
So how do we get there?

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:24.640
As a reminder, there
are three simple steps

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:27.250
you need take to
be able to achieve

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:29.440
these goals for yourself.

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:32.717
One, remember to
take big swings.

00:11:32.717 --> 00:11:35.050
Don't take a minimum detectable
effect that's too small.

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:37.900
That's a huge lever to be able
to make your experiment shorter

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:39.430
right off the bat.

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:42.550
Two, use sequential testing.

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:44.440
Now, that you know
how, it can be

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:47.710
simple to just implement and
overlay on top of your own A/B

00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:49.460
tests.

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:55.430
And three, using our Shiny
flexdashboard or your own tool

00:11:55.430 --> 00:11:58.130
of choice to be
able to demonstrate

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:00.560
how to do this to everyone
in your organization

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:03.470
and share the goodness that
is sequential testing, so

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:06.350
that everyone, even
non-data scientists,

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:10.040
can run shorter, more
statistically rigorous tests.

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:13.310
Now, with just those three tips
and tricks in your tool belt,

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:17.900
you can avoid early
significant results,

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:22.100
disappointing your antsy boss,
and business constraints.

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:23.630
Because now, you
have all the tools

00:12:23.630 --> 00:12:30.230
you need to be able to run your
tests shorter and reliably,

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:33.920
so that everyone in the
organization can feel confident

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:35.860
in your results.

